// TWO NAMED REGIONS OF ONE BINDER, MENTIONED IN NO `where` CLAUSE, ARE
// UNRELATED — the refuse half of the 2026-08-31h narrowing in
// include/logos/compiler/outlives.hpp (`current_lt_binders()`).
//
// `mk` declares BOTH 'b and 'a and relates them nowhere, so storing a `&'b i64`
// in a `Holder<'a>` is E0621 upstream. `outlives()` used to answer TRUE for two
// named regions that appear nowhere in the outlives graph, on the theory that
// the caller's region inference would unify them — but a BINDER of the callee
// is not the caller's to choose.
//
// ⚠ ITS TWIN IS ONE TOKEN AWAY, AND IT NO LONGER GETS AWAY WITH IT. Renaming
// the STRUCT's binder from 'a to 'h used to MISS this program, because the
// 2026-08-31h rule worked by NAME COLLISION and not by substitution. The
// elision engine landed on 2026-08-31n and the twin moved with it:
// tests/logos/fail/bc_ltunmentbind_renamed_binder_hole is now REFUSED, with the
// same diagnostic this file pins. Retired here rather than silently deleted, as
// that file's own header asked.
// ⚠ `.expected` RE-PINNED 2026-08-31n (rule 14 EXEMPTION, stated). rc is 1
// before and after; the refusal MOVED to the type checker's return-type
// variance comparison, where the elision engine and the region slot make the
// callee's binder and the caller's actually comparable. The text it replaces
// is kept here so the move is readable rather than lost:
//     struct literal 'Holder' field 'v': variance mismatch — expected &'a i64, got &'b i64 — lifetime structure incompatible (check &mut invariance / contravariance rules)
// A `.expected` LOSS IS A HYPOTHESIS, and its LEGAL TWIN is the instrument
// that tests it: tests/logos/pass/bc_ltcallmeet_expected_loss_twins.logos
// carries this row's twin, one construct apart. PROBES.md 2026-08-31n.
package bc_ltunmentbind_two_unrelated_binders;

struct Holder<'a> { v: &'a i64 }

fn mk<'b, 'a>(y: &'b i64) -> Holder<'a> {
    return Holder { v: y };
}

fn main() -> i32 {
    return 0i32;
}
